# Comprehensive Survey of KEGG Pathways and GO Biological Processes Up‑Regulated or Down‑Regulated in Myocardial Ischemia‑Reperfusion Injury (MIRI)

## Abstract
Transcriptomic profiling of myocardial ischemia‑reperfusion injury (MIRI) has repeatedly identified massive transcriptional reprogramming that underpins the shift from ischemic tolerance to inflammatory‑driven tissue damage. Through a systematic synthesis of recent bioinformatics studies, functional enrichment analyses, and experimental validations (2025‑2026), this report catalogues at least **30 up‑regulated** and **30 down‑regulated** KEGG pathways and Gene Ontology (GO) biological processes. Inflammatory signalling cascades – TNF, NF‑κB, IL‑17, JAK‑STAT, NOD‑like, and Toll‑like receptor pathways – dominate the up‑regulated landscape, alongside processes such as neutrophil chemotaxis, cytokine production, oxidative stress responses, and coagulation. Conversely, mitochondrial oxidative metabolism, ion transport, cardiac muscle contraction, and calcium signalling are consistently down‑regulated. The integration of multiple data sources reveals a cohesive picture of MIRI as a hyper‑inflammatory, metabolic‑suppressive state that offers numerous diagnostic and therapeutic entry points.

## Introduction
Myocardial ischemia‑reperfusion injury (MIRI) occurs when abrupt restoration of blood flow to ischemic myocardium paradoxically exacerbates tissue damage through sterile inflammation, calcium overload, mitochondrial dysfunction, and oxidative burst. High‑throughput transcriptomic analyses have uncovered thousands of differentially expressed genes (DEGs), often with a pronounced preponderance of up‑regulated transcripts that code for pro‑inflammatory mediators, alarmins, and cell‑death executors ([MDPI Biomedicines 2025](https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9059/13/9/2114)). Comprehensive pathway‑level mapping of these expression shifts is essential for identifying the dominant molecular programmes that drive injury and for selecting rational therapeutic targets.

Over the past two years, multiple independent investigations have applied Gene Ontology (GO) and Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) enrichment to MIRI datasets, frequently separating up‑ and down‑regulated gene sets to distil the directionality of biological processes. The present survey amalgamates these findings to provide a coherent reference atlas of pathways activated or suppressed during reperfusion injury, each supported by concrete transcriptomic and, in many cases, experimental evidence.

## Methodology of Literature Aggregation
The compilation draws on original research articles and bioinformatics studies identified through a deep literature crawl. Only studies that explicitly performed KEGG and/or GO enrichment on DEGs separated by direction of change (up‑ or down‑regulation) were considered. Key sources include:
- A cross‑organ transcriptomic meta‑analysis of ischemia‑reperfusion injury that separately examined up‑ and down‑regulated genes across multiple organs, using GO and KEGG ([MDPI cross‑comparison](https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9059/13/9/2114)).
- A 2026 *Scientific Reports* article validating NF‑κB pathway‑related biomarkers (*Nfkbia* and *Icam1*) in MIRI and describing pathway enrichment for up‑regulated candidates ([Ting et al., 2026](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-47878-9)).
- A 2025 *Frontiers in Immunology* study on metabolic‑syndrome‑associated MIRI that provided diagnostic hub genes and immune‑pathway enrichment ([Liu S. et al., 2025](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1567572/full)).
- A temporal transcriptomic analysis of cardiac ischemia‑reperfusion injury that performed GO and KEGG enrichment at multiple reperfusion time points ([Frontiers in Physiology, 2025](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/physiology/articles/10.3389/fphys.2025.1668586/full)).
- Additional bioinformatics studies focusing on S100A9‑mediated RAGE/NF‑κB/NLRP3 activation ([Liu Y. et al., 2025](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s41065-025-00397-5)), CTRP9‑associated hub genes, and comparative MI/I/R expression shifts.

When original publications did not explicitly list KEGG/GO IDs, standard identifiers from the KEGG database and Gene Ontology Consortium were assigned to the described pathway names. Every entry is accompanied by the regulation direction (UP = activated/enriched in up‑regulated genes, DOWN = suppressed/enriched in down‑regulated genes) and the supporting citation(s). The tables below present a balanced selection of KEGG pathways and GO biological processes (at least 30 in each category).

## Results

### Up‑Regulated Pathways in MIRI
